WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:02.700 --> 00:00:04.320
Hello this is Peter Cooper.

00:00:04.320 --> 00:00:09.410
Welcome to today's NCBI Minute webinar on
the SRA RunSelector.

00:00:09.410 --> 00:00:13.129
The answers and questions that are asked during
the webinar will be available in a document

00:00:13.129 --> 00:00:16.070
that will be linked to the webinars page.

00:00:16.070 --> 00:00:21.020
There's also a materials directory on the
ftp site that has Ashley's slides.

00:00:21.020 --> 00:00:25.050
And this compressed URL will take you there.

00:00:25.050 --> 00:00:30.260
So I'd like to introduce Ashley Hintz who
will talk to you about the SRA run selector.

00:00:30.260 --> 00:00:33.510
All right, thank you very much.

00:00:33.510 --> 00:00:36.630
We will go ahead and get started.

00:00:36.630 --> 00:00:38.840
What are we going to talk about today?

00:00:38.840 --> 00:00:45.579
We will talk about the structure of the SRA
database, what the run selector is, how to

00:00:45.579 --> 00:00:52.250
use the filters, getting access to the data,
and we will go through a couple of examples

00:00:52.250 --> 00:00:56.399
of the SRA toolkit command.

00:00:56.399 --> 00:01:00.989
This is a diagram of a standard SRA submission
set up.

00:01:00.989 --> 00:01:08.939
At the top we have two studies that were submitted,
those are directly linked to the BioProject

00:01:08.939 --> 00:01:13.420
and they will also have an SRP accession number
in SRA.

00:01:13.420 --> 00:01:19.500
Each one of these studies has multiple samples
linked to it and these are directly linked

00:01:19.500 --> 00:01:22.939
to the registered BioSample.

00:01:22.939 --> 00:01:25.970
They will also have a unique SRS accession.

00:01:25.970 --> 00:01:32.490
You can see there's one sample in the middle,
it is called the control, it is party to both

00:01:32.490 --> 00:01:33.860
studies.

00:01:33.860 --> 00:01:35.799
That is perfectly acceptable.

00:01:35.799 --> 00:01:42.310
Underneath the study and the samples we have
two more categories, Experiment and Run.

00:01:42.310 --> 00:01:46.039
This information is directly stored in SRA.

00:01:46.039 --> 00:01:52.749
Experiment accessions with the SRX number,
have all of the metadata provided by the researcher

00:01:52.749 --> 00:01:55.180
contained within it.

00:01:55.180 --> 00:02:01.299
Underneath that we have the SRR number which
is the run accession.

00:02:01.299 --> 00:02:06.390
This is where the actual data files for the
sequence data are stored.

00:02:06.390 --> 00:02:13.379
You can see in some of the samples, one experiment
linked to multiple runs and some samples have

00:02:13.379 --> 00:02:15.540
multiple experiments and multiple runs.

00:02:15.540 --> 00:02:22.160
There are different ways to set up SRA to
fit your research project.

00:02:22.160 --> 00:02:23.540
What is run selector?

00:02:23.540 --> 00:02:28.349
It is a table view of you of SRA data available
from your search terms.

00:02:28.349 --> 00:02:33.100
We'll go through a demonstration of this after
I lay out the basics.

00:02:33.100 --> 00:02:38.470
This allows for more precise filtering of
the SRA metadata, it collates the common columns

00:02:38.470 --> 00:02:44.319
of data between the projects, with a couple
of exceptions, there has to be a 10% similarity

00:02:44.319 --> 00:02:50.290
between the data columns and fields with large
number of characters will not be included

00:02:50.290 --> 00:02:52.879
because it makes the table harder to read.

00:02:52.879 --> 00:02:58.569
There is a limit of 20,000 run accessions
that can be viewed.

00:02:58.569 --> 00:03:03.280
Finally after you have filtered down the data
to where you want, you can export the data

00:03:03.280 --> 00:03:07.379
accessions and the metadata as a spreadsheet.

00:03:07.379 --> 00:03:09.200
Let's jump into the demo.

00:03:09.200 --> 00:03:13.060
We will start from the main NCBI page.

00:03:13.060 --> 00:03:16.780
I have SRA database selected.

00:03:16.780 --> 00:03:20.359
This is where you can enter in any search
term.

00:03:20.359 --> 00:03:26.200
Be sure the search term is relatively specific
so you get below that 20,000 run accession

00:03:26.200 --> 00:03:27.680
limit.

00:03:27.680 --> 00:03:30.370
I am interested in ancient DNA.

00:03:30.370 --> 00:03:34.400
Click Enter.

00:03:34.400 --> 00:03:41.769
This will take me to all of the SRA experiment
accessions that we have for the search terms.

00:03:41.769 --> 00:03:45.090
On the left hand side you can see there is
an Access area.

00:03:45.090 --> 00:03:49.790
I want to point out that you can use the run
selector on dbGaP data.

00:03:49.790 --> 00:03:52.000
In this case we are only going to look at
public data.

00:03:52.000 --> 00:03:54.099
I'm gonna go ahead and click that.

00:03:54.099 --> 00:04:00.079
You can see there is a box at the top that
asks me if I want to send this data to the

00:04:00.079 --> 00:04:01.180
run selector.

00:04:01.180 --> 00:04:02.560
Which I do.

00:04:02.560 --> 00:04:05.180
So I'll go ahead and click that.

00:04:05.180 --> 00:04:09.290
After it caches the data you will see a view
like this.

00:04:09.290 --> 00:04:14.120
You'll notice on the left-hand side there
is a box labeled, Facets.

00:04:14.120 --> 00:04:21.670
This is all of the data column headers that
you can use to filter the table.

00:04:21.670 --> 00:04:26.140
You will notice that the Center is automatically
selected.

00:04:26.140 --> 00:04:29.450
This is the default.

00:04:29.450 --> 00:04:35.890
If you don't care which center the data came
from, you can click on the red minus button

00:04:35.890 --> 00:04:40.310
and it will make it go away.

00:04:40.310 --> 00:04:44.350
Now I can filter the data the way I am interested.

00:04:44.350 --> 00:04:47.860
We will start with organism.

00:04:47.860 --> 00:04:53.370
As you can see we have lots of different data,
for multiple organisms.

00:04:53.370 --> 00:04:56.390
I am interested in human data.

00:04:56.390 --> 00:04:59.820
I'm gonna type in that genus.

00:04:59.820 --> 00:05:03.980
There are two different species for the genus
Homo.

00:05:03.980 --> 00:05:06.590
We have Homo sapiens, of course, and also
Homo heidelgergensis.

00:05:06.590 --> 00:05:11.810
I am interested in Homo sapiens so we'll click
on that one, and you notice that the table

00:05:11.810 --> 00:05:13.010
automatically updates.

00:05:13.010 --> 00:05:18.860
I had over 2000 runs and now I have 486.

00:05:18.860 --> 00:05:24.330
The next category I am interested in looking
at is the Assay type.

00:05:24.330 --> 00:05:27.680
I'll click on that.

00:05:27.680 --> 00:05:34.210
It also identifies how many runs are labeled
as each of these categories.

00:05:34.210 --> 00:05:39.431
I am interested in whole genome sequencing,
so we'll click on that category, and again

00:05:39.431 --> 00:05:40.900
my table will update.

00:05:40.900 --> 00:05:43.090
I'll make a couple more selections here.

00:05:43.090 --> 00:05:47.860
I will select a particular library.

00:05:47.860 --> 00:05:51.170
We have hybrid selection, unspecified, and
random.

00:05:51.170 --> 00:05:56.180
I am specifically interested in random.

00:05:56.180 --> 00:05:59.650
Finally I want a specific spot length.

00:05:59.650 --> 00:06:06.890
Let's type in 60; I want them at least 60.

00:06:06.890 --> 00:06:11.840
Once I type that in, you can see there are
only three categories that fit that criterion.

00:06:11.840 --> 00:06:13.790
Select all three of those.

00:06:13.790 --> 00:06:16.710
Each time I do this it will update.

00:06:16.710 --> 00:06:21.380
Now I have 131 runs that fit the criteria
that I am interested in.

00:06:21.380 --> 00:06:29.020
You will notice not all the accessions in
the table start with that SRR letters.

00:06:29.020 --> 00:06:31.740
These begin with an ERR.

00:06:31.740 --> 00:06:37.090
That means it is coming from our European
database, our counterparts in Europe.

00:06:37.090 --> 00:06:43.810
Our database mirrors with European and Japanese
databases for next generation sequencing data,

00:06:43.810 --> 00:06:47.920
so when you search our database you are not
only looking at SRA data, you are also looking

00:06:47.920 --> 00:06:50.300
at data from those two organizations.

00:06:50.300 --> 00:06:53.390
You're getting three databases.

00:06:53.390 --> 00:06:56.860
I can sort each one of these columns.

00:06:56.860 --> 00:07:01.050
If I click on it, it's going to sort by the
letters and numbers.

00:07:01.050 --> 00:07:03.750
So forwards, then backwards.

00:07:03.750 --> 00:07:06.720
Then I can see all those SRR accessions.

00:07:06.720 --> 00:07:13.000
In this case there are not very many because
ancient DNA is more prevalent in Europe, for

00:07:13.000 --> 00:07:14.830
doing research.

00:07:14.830 --> 00:07:17.960
That's why you see a lot more ERR accessions.

00:07:17.960 --> 00:07:23.960
You will notice the first four columns are
blue hyperlinks.

00:07:23.960 --> 00:07:28.610
I can click on one of these and it will send
me to the information for that particular

00:07:28.610 --> 00:07:31.970
run, study, bioproject or sample.

00:07:31.970 --> 00:07:37.290
This one looks interesting, just because it
says ancient DNA of a metagenome.

00:07:37.290 --> 00:07:41.620
Right click and open that in a new tab.

00:07:41.620 --> 00:07:48.440
This takes me to all of the run data and I
can see how many spots it has, the size, the

00:07:48.440 --> 00:07:54.160
GC content and at the bottom I can see the
title for this project, it is specifically

00:07:54.160 --> 00:07:57.850
from a tooth from a victim of the Black Death.

00:07:57.850 --> 00:08:02.230
That's kind of cool.

00:08:02.230 --> 00:08:07.960
Let's just scroll out here so I can show you
a couple more things.

00:08:07.960 --> 00:08:13.310
You can see for some of these categories there
are runs that are blank, the researchers chose

00:08:13.310 --> 00:08:16.720
not to enter information for that particular
category.

00:08:16.720 --> 00:08:20.580
Keep that in mind while you're filtering the
data sets and looking through them, the data

00:08:20.580 --> 00:08:27.200
are provided to us from research communities
so not all of the columns will be filled in.

00:08:27.200 --> 00:08:34.469
In this case we are doing pretty good; there
are some missing collection dates.

00:08:34.469 --> 00:08:40.120
Let's say all of the data looks good, I want
to export it.

00:08:40.120 --> 00:08:44.410
Click the green plus button and it will select
everything in that spreadsheet.

00:08:44.410 --> 00:08:51.900
Maybe I am not interested in that black death
victim, so I can unselect that particular

00:08:51.900 --> 00:08:52.900
run.

00:08:52.900 --> 00:09:02.330
And you can see under our selected column,
I have 130 runs selected, and I will download

00:09:02.330 --> 00:09:03.330
that information.

00:09:03.330 --> 00:09:15.000
Okay, it's under Downloads; let's see if I
can find it.

00:09:15.000 --> 00:09:33.290
Now I want to open that in Excel.

00:09:33.290 --> 00:09:38.620
Now here I have to make sure I select All
files because when you select to download

00:09:38.620 --> 00:09:44.870
the run table information it will download
as a tab-delimited text file.

00:09:44.870 --> 00:09:46.510
Make sure you select All files.

00:09:46.510 --> 00:09:48.510
Choose that it is delimited.

00:09:48.510 --> 00:09:50.310
And I have to choose that it is tab-delmimited.

00:09:50.310 --> 00:09:51.310
Again Next.

00:09:51.310 --> 00:09:53.180
Then click finish.

00:09:53.180 --> 00:09:59.800
I'll make the columns a bit wider.

00:09:59.800 --> 00:10:05.740
Now you can see here is that same information
that was in the run selector, it is now in

00:10:05.740 --> 00:10:11.250
my spreadsheet and I can save it, make notes,
and come back to it later.

00:10:11.250 --> 00:10:16.050
The other option for downloading the information
is just to download a list of the run accessions.

00:10:16.050 --> 00:10:20.140
This is the most important accession number
that you will get from this table, because

00:10:20.140 --> 00:10:26.330
as previously stated the actual data files
are resting under the run accession numbers.

00:10:26.330 --> 00:10:29.920
We will download our accession list.

00:10:29.920 --> 00:10:36.970
I will open this in notepad plus plus.

00:10:36.970 --> 00:10:42.090
Okay, here are all my run accessions.

00:10:42.090 --> 00:10:43.980
All 130.

00:10:43.980 --> 00:10:49.500
All right, let's jump back into our slideshow
here.

00:10:49.500 --> 00:10:54.400
Now you have all this metadata information
and all of these run accessions, how do you

00:10:54.400 --> 00:10:56.630
get access to the actual data files.

00:10:56.630 --> 00:10:59.330
You will use the SRA toolkit.

00:10:59.330 --> 00:11:04.880
This is a commandline application that works
on all current 64-bit systems; Windows, Mac

00:11:04.880 --> 00:11:06.970
and we have two versions of Linux.

00:11:06.970 --> 00:11:11.300
You can download the data in multiple data
formats, the most common being fastq.

00:11:11.300 --> 00:11:17.440
For any aligned data you can also download
bam files, and we also have SFF files, among

00:11:17.440 --> 00:11:18.660
others.

00:11:18.660 --> 00:11:21.800
You can limit the amount of data that you
download.

00:11:21.800 --> 00:11:25.610
Maybe you are only intersted in chromosome
1; maybe you're only interested in the back

00:11:25.610 --> 00:11:27.490
half of chromosome 16.

00:11:27.490 --> 00:11:33.560
You can enter in those sections when downloading
the data so you don't have to obtain the entire

00:11:33.560 --> 00:11:35.910
large data file.

00:11:35.910 --> 00:11:39.540
Another thing the SRA Tooliket does is BLAST.

00:11:39.540 --> 00:11:46.910
You can blast data from SRA including barcodes
or aligned slices.

00:11:46.910 --> 00:11:48.570
And that's just the beginning.

00:11:48.570 --> 00:11:54.190
Here are a couple examples of commands you
can use in the SRA toolkit that you can use

00:11:54.190 --> 00:11:56.339
to access data.

00:11:56.339 --> 00:12:02.000
For a standard fastq download you would use
fastq-dump and the SRR number.

00:12:02.000 --> 00:12:08.080
This function includes options for splitting
files if it's paired data, and also only downloading

00:12:08.080 --> 00:12:11.310
a section of the file.

00:12:11.310 --> 00:12:17.420
If you want to download a bam file, make sure
you have to filter by aligned data and you

00:12:17.420 --> 00:12:21.020
need to have samtools installed on your computer
because they work together.

00:12:21.020 --> 00:12:27.260
You're going to use sam-dump and the SRR number
to get your sam file, then take it directly

00:12:27.260 --> 00:12:31.460
into samtools to convert it into your bam
file.

00:12:31.460 --> 00:12:35.180
Then finally an example BLAST command.

00:12:35.180 --> 00:12:36.220
So it's blastn_vdb.

00:12:36.220 --> 00:12:45.880
And in this case there are three different
run accessions we're querying against.

00:12:45.880 --> 00:12:50.779
The other thing to point out is this nt.test
file; this is the actual sequence you are

00:12:50.779 --> 00:12:51.990
searching for.

00:12:51.990 --> 00:12:56.200
So here is the nucleotide sequence to see
if it's in any of these run accessions.

00:12:56.200 --> 00:12:59.110
And then dot out will give you the results.

00:12:59.110 --> 00:13:04.910
Here are the links for all the information
that we talked about today, including some

00:13:04.910 --> 00:13:08.440
additional information about the SRA database.

00:13:08.440 --> 00:13:15.210
And with that I'm happy to take questions
Just to remind you while we are open like

00:13:15.210 --> 00:13:19.810
this, if you have any questions about any
of the resources you heard about today, or

00:13:19.810 --> 00:13:29.270
any NCBI resources in general, you can write
to our info address, info@ncbi.nlm.nih.gov,

00:13:29.270 --> 00:13:31.240
that's the same domain name as our web page.

00:13:31.240 --> 00:13:39.290
If you have questions about the webinars program
or webinars in general, please write to the

00:13:39.290 --> 00:13:42.370
webinars address, webinars@ncbi.nlm.nih.gov.

00:13:42.370 --> 00:13:48.310
I don't see any questions that we can answer
right now.

00:13:48.310 --> 00:13:50.410
Thank you very much for attending the webinar
today.

00:13:50.410 --> 00:13:51.970
That is all for today.

00:13:51.970 --> 00:13:58.160
We will see you next week on the 30th for
a webinar about the NCBI Hackathons.

00:13:58.160 --> 00:13:59.160
Thanks a lot.

00:13:59.160 --> 00:14:00.160
Thank you for coming.

00:14:00.160 --> 00:14:00.660
Thanks Ashley.

